1242
00:56:17.620 --> 00:56:21.700
And throughout the day, she said, I texted Rain.

1243
00:56:21.940 --> 00:56:25.300
Rain actually thinks it was an alt pitched from Paul.

1244
00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:28.000
Jen said, here's a side note.

1245
00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:33.740
I had a friend that reabsorbed his twin, but I definitely didn't come up with the strength of a baby.

1246
00:56:35.240 --> 00:56:37.980
She said, I'm going to check with Paul where that part came from.

1247
00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:38.900
Please hold.

1248
00:56:39.160 --> 00:56:39.920
She texts Paul.

1249
00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:42.820
Paul says he thinks it was Justin Spitzer.

1250
00:56:43.120 --> 00:56:44.020
Hold one second.

1251
00:56:44.320 --> 00:56:45.440
Now texting Justin.

1252
00:56:45.720 --> 00:56:47.160
Justin was another one of our writers.

1253
00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:49.920
This is what Justin texted Jen back.

1254
00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:55.580
I still remember that because it was early season three and I was so nervous about proving myself.

1255
00:56:55.880 --> 00:56:58.140
I guess, yes, I came up with it.

1256
00:56:58.220 --> 00:57:05.440
But the thing that really sold it to me was the last line about him having the strength of a grown man plus a baby.

1257
00:57:05.440 --> 00:57:09.100
And that was something that Paul Lieberstein added after.

1258
00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:12.400
So I think he deserves a fair amount of credit as well.

1259
00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:17.420
So I'm giving that a slow clap.

1260
00:57:17.440 --> 00:57:18.060
A slow clap.

1261
00:57:18.260 --> 00:57:20.020
Into a big clap.

1262
00:57:20.500 --> 00:57:20.820
Yes.

1263
00:57:21.680 --> 00:57:22.240
Wow.

1264
00:57:22.540 --> 00:57:25.040
It was a two-hander that Paul and Justin pitched.

1265
00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:31.300
I love that we got to the bottom of that mystery because it is one of my all-time favorite Dwight-isMs.

1266
00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:36.660
Also, guys, Justin Spitzer is the creator of the show Superstore.

1267
00:57:37.000 --> 00:57:39.080
Justin is so talented.

1268
00:57:39.340 --> 00:57:40.240
He's so talented.

1269
00:57:40.240 --> 00:57:40.840
I love him.

1270
00:57:40.960 --> 00:57:41.380
Love him.

1271
00:57:41.900 --> 00:57:42.420
Anyway.

1272
00:57:42.760 --> 00:57:47.360
I think the only place to go from here is back to our grief circle.

1273
00:57:47.760 --> 00:57:49.960
And we need to hear from some others.

1274
00:57:50.420 --> 00:57:50.680
Okay.

1275
00:57:50.760 --> 00:57:53.200
So we're back into the grief counseling scene.

1276
00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:54.680
And Stanley has just refused.

1277
00:57:54.680 --> 00:57:56.280
He is not going to participate.

1278
00:57:56.540 --> 00:57:56.680
No.

1279
00:57:57.120 --> 00:57:58.900
He chunks that ball back at Michael.

1280
00:57:59.420 --> 00:57:59.800
Hey.

1281
00:57:59.900 --> 00:58:00.500
I heard that.

1282
00:58:00.640 --> 00:58:01.260
Yeah, you heard that.

1283
00:58:01.260 --> 00:58:03.840
But Pam agrees to participate.

1284
00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:06.840
And she's going to share a story about her family.

1285
00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:11.380
But it's clearly just the synopsis for the movie Million Dollar Baby.

1286
00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:12.340
Yeah.

1287
00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:14.400
I mean, again, Pam is now the gem.

1288
00:58:14.660 --> 00:58:15.480
She's the jokester.

1289
00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:16.700
Yes.

1290
00:58:16.900 --> 00:58:22.680
And then Ryan joins in and he shares a story that is very clearly the Lion King.

1291
00:58:22.980 --> 00:58:24.820
I mean, he says the word Mufasa.

1292
00:58:25.100 --> 00:58:26.580
I mean, come on.

1293
00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:30.360
But then Kevin catches on and he's tickled.

1294
00:58:30.560 --> 00:58:31.540
He wants to participate.

1295
00:58:31.540 --> 00:58:32.840
He wants to play the game.

1296
00:58:32.960 --> 00:58:35.400
But he goes too broad, too on the nose.

1297
00:58:35.560 --> 00:58:37.980
And he basically breaks down Weekend at Bernie's.

1298
00:58:38.080 --> 00:58:39.740
And Michael's like, what?

1299
00:58:39.920 --> 00:58:40.600
Hold up.

1300
00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:41.860
That's a movie.

1301
00:58:42.160 --> 00:58:43.440
And he gets really ticked off.

1302
00:58:43.620 --> 00:58:43.760
Okay.

1303
00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:48.580
So there was a lot of curiosity about these movie plots in the conference room.

1304
00:58:48.740 --> 00:58:52.080
We got a fan question from Tristan who wants to know,

1305
00:58:52.220 --> 00:58:55.060
when everyone is in the conference room for Michael's counseling session,

1306
00:58:55.060 --> 00:58:59.580
did the cast improvise the movie plots that were mentioned or were they scripted?

1307
00:58:59.740 --> 00:59:01.700
Jenna, to my knowledge, they were all scripted.

1308
00:59:01.840 --> 00:59:02.860
That's how I remember it.

1309
00:59:03.540 --> 00:59:03.840
Yes.

1310
00:59:03.980 --> 00:59:05.480
I looked these up in the shooting draft.

1311
00:59:05.740 --> 00:59:12.020
And Pam's Million Dollar Baby story and Kevin's Weekend at Bernie story are both in the script.

1312
00:59:12.200 --> 00:59:15.840
However, Ryan's fake movie story is different.

1313
00:59:16.280 --> 00:59:16.660
Ah.

1314
00:59:17.380 --> 00:59:17.840
Yes.

1315
00:59:17.840 --> 00:59:21.140
In the script, he talks about Thelma and Louise.

1316
00:59:22.100 --> 00:59:22.340
Well.

1317
00:59:22.340 --> 00:59:23.560
This is what he says.

1318
00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:24.860
He says,

1319
00:59:25.300 --> 00:59:28.240
My mom's sister Thelma died last year.

1320
00:59:28.880 --> 00:59:31.520
She was with her friend named Lucy.

1321
00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:34.920
A cop was trying to pull them over.

1322
00:59:35.080 --> 00:59:39.400
And I guess the brakes must have not been working because they ended up driving over a cliff.

1323
00:59:39.580 --> 00:59:41.680
And it was a total shock to everyone.

1324
00:59:42.140 --> 00:59:42.500
Okay.

1325
00:59:42.760 --> 00:59:45.220
Well, we wanted to get to the bottom of this.

1326
00:59:45.380 --> 00:59:49.980
So we reached out to the source, the writer of this episode, Jen Salata.

1327
00:59:50.260 --> 00:59:52.160
And this is what she had to say.

1328
00:59:52.160 --> 00:59:56.920
For the movies and the grief counseling session, I was really excited.

1329
00:59:56.920 --> 00:59:58.600
It was really fun to think about.

1330
00:59:58.900 --> 01:00:09.220
I went off on script and needed to find a way that everybody would sit there with Michael Scott and not share their own personal stories of huge loss.

1331
01:00:09.220 --> 01:00:12.040
Because it just didn't seem like they would do that.

1332
01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:18.260
So I thought they should share movies plot lines as if they were their own lives.

1333
01:00:18.260 --> 01:00:23.640
And I came up with Million Dollar Baby for you, Jenna, for Pam.

1334
01:00:23.800 --> 01:00:28.400
And then also for Kevin, Weekend at Bernie's.

1335
01:00:28.400 --> 01:00:31.420
And for Ryan, I had a different one.

1336
01:00:31.820 --> 01:00:34.280
I think BJ came up with The Lion King.

1337
01:00:34.540 --> 01:00:36.460
I don't quite remember, but I think it was BJ.

1338
01:00:36.760 --> 01:00:37.940
And I love that one.

1339
01:00:38.520 --> 01:00:42.120
The one I had, I actually found my first draft of the script.

1340
01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:43.760
I had Ryan.

1341
01:00:43.760 --> 01:00:45.000
I'll read you the line.

1342
01:00:45.200 --> 01:00:46.780
It's not as good as The Lion King one.

1343
01:00:46.780 --> 01:00:49.980
But I have Ryan say, this is almost too horrible to talk about.

1344
01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:52.080
But I had a cousin who was a big city lawyer.

1345
01:00:52.080 --> 01:00:54.660
And he was visiting an Amish town not far from here.

1346
01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:56.940
And he was smothered to death in their grain silo.

1347
01:00:57.220 --> 01:01:00.000
And then I had Michael say, ooh, that must have made you really sad.

1348
01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:00.420
Did it?

1349
01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:01.340
And Ryan says, yes.

1350
01:01:01.700 --> 01:01:04.420
So I think that was a witness.

1351
01:01:04.620 --> 01:01:05.740
So I had Ryan's from Witness.

1352
01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:10.620
I wish I could remember some of the other ones because I feel like we had other ones that we pitched on.

1353
01:01:10.740 --> 01:01:13.240
But those were the ones from my first draft.

1354
01:01:13.240 --> 01:01:16.080
And then, of course, the Lion King circle of life was fantastic.

1355
01:01:16.080 --> 01:01:20.820
And Ryan saying, it'll take about an hour and a half to tell it was like such a great line.

1356
01:01:21.800 --> 01:01:23.000
That is amazing.

1357
01:01:23.180 --> 01:01:25.540
By the way, my college roommate and I used to watch Witness.

1358
01:01:25.900 --> 01:01:29.500
And we would say to each other all the time, be careful out among them English.

1359
01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:31.640
Thank you.

1360
01:01:31.960 --> 01:01:34.620
That is my movie quote from Witness.

1361
01:01:35.020 --> 01:01:39.220
But isn't that wild, Jenna, that Ryan's movie went through three versions.

1362
01:01:39.220 --> 01:01:40.000
It was Witness.

1363
01:01:40.000 --> 01:01:43.080
And then it was, I guess, Thelma and Louise, according to your script.

1364
01:01:43.240 --> 01:01:45.840
But The Lion King is what made it in the show.

1365
01:01:46.740 --> 01:01:51.480
Yeah, clearly they had a lot of alts for these movie plots.

1366
01:01:51.520 --> 01:01:54.220
But all of them were scripted, guys.

1367
01:01:54.460 --> 01:01:56.380
All of them written by our awesome writers.

1368
01:01:56.560 --> 01:01:58.140
We did not improvise that stuff.

1369
01:01:58.560 --> 01:01:59.400
Love that.

1370
01:01:59.500 --> 01:02:01.460
Love that little tidbit, Jen Salata.

1371
01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:07.560
So, guys, Michael gets pretty upset that people are not taking this seriously.

1372
01:02:08.100 --> 01:02:14.600
You know, he says the guy who used to do his job and sit at his desk has died and no one cares.

1373
01:02:14.940 --> 01:02:15.100
Yeah.

1374
01:02:16.200 --> 01:02:21.260
And Toby tries to talk him down and tries to explain that this is just life.

1375
01:02:21.260 --> 01:02:26.400
And that this morning he saw a bird fly into the window downstairs and die.

1376
01:02:27.400 --> 01:02:28.540
Michael freaks out.

1377
01:02:28.920 --> 01:02:29.700
Oh, my gosh.

1378
01:02:29.820 --> 01:02:30.080
Toby.

1379
01:02:30.360 --> 01:02:31.680
Toby, what are you thinking?

1380
01:02:32.040 --> 01:02:34.120
He totally attaches to that.

1381
01:02:34.420 --> 01:02:38.220
Now Michael's grief for Ed is somehow wrapped into this bird.

1382
01:02:38.220 --> 01:02:40.040
It's going to be a whole thing.

1383
01:02:40.380 --> 01:02:40.600
Yeah.

1384
01:02:40.780 --> 01:02:42.100
He runs downstairs.

1385
01:02:42.160 --> 01:02:43.440
He freaks out.

1386
01:02:43.600 --> 01:02:45.280
He wants to see if the bird is alive.

1387
01:02:46.080 --> 01:02:48.560
I had a fan catch here from Kendra.

1388
01:02:49.460 --> 01:02:56.040
At 14 minutes, 33 seconds, when Michael runs through the front door to get the bird, he runs past the security desk.

1389
01:02:56.200 --> 01:02:58.440
And the security guard is not Hank.

1390
01:02:58.720 --> 01:03:00.680
Oh, good catch.

1391
01:03:01.220 --> 01:03:03.180
Yeah, different security guard.

1392
01:03:03.340 --> 01:03:05.200
Hank took a personal day, you guys.

1393
01:03:05.200 --> 01:03:06.260
I guess so.

1394
01:03:06.400 --> 01:03:09.500
Well, Michael attempts to resuscitate the bird in the kitchen.

1395
01:03:10.140 --> 01:03:11.520
Angela, you're not pleased.

1396
01:03:11.880 --> 01:03:14.520
So Michael is determined to save this bird.

1397
01:03:14.720 --> 01:03:16.140
He brings it into the kitchen.

1398
01:03:16.640 --> 01:03:18.860
Angela Martin is not pleased.

1399
01:03:19.100 --> 01:03:21.000
She's like, no, that shouldn't be in here.

1400
01:03:21.340 --> 01:03:21.980
Get it out.

1401
01:03:22.220 --> 01:03:23.580
Well, she is not pleased.

1402
01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:24.820
And Angela, she's not wrong.

1403
01:03:25.080 --> 01:03:25.980
Thank you.

1404
01:03:26.240 --> 01:03:27.260
Thank you, Jenna.

1405
01:03:27.820 --> 01:03:28.580
You're welcome.

1406
01:03:28.880 --> 01:03:29.640
You're welcome.

1407
01:03:29.880 --> 01:03:32.700
We had a fan question from Ellie Friedlander.

1408
01:03:32.700 --> 01:03:36.340
She wants to know, was that a real dead bird?

1409
01:03:37.540 --> 01:03:38.180
No.

1410
01:03:38.420 --> 01:03:39.280
No, no.

1411
01:03:39.280 --> 01:03:39.840
No, Ellie.

1412
01:03:40.080 --> 01:03:42.000
It was a fake bird.

1413
01:03:42.200 --> 01:03:43.880
I reached out to Phil.

1414
01:03:44.120 --> 01:03:51.640
He told me that he found three dead fake bird options and presented them to Jen, Roger, our director, and Greg.

1415
01:03:51.640 --> 01:03:54.280
They chose the one that you see in the show.

1416
01:03:54.460 --> 01:04:03.120
And Angela, I have to say, when he texted me back that he presented the three dead birds, I imagined in my head that they were on a silver tray.

1417
01:04:03.500 --> 01:04:07.340
Because remember, Phil would always present you your props on a silver tray.

1418
01:04:07.740 --> 01:04:08.380
Yes, yes.

1419
01:04:08.380 --> 01:04:10.840
And I was just imagining it.

1420
01:04:10.840 --> 01:04:14.040
And I wrote him back and I said, Phil, were they on a silver tray?

1421
01:04:14.040 --> 01:04:15.820
And he said, you know me too well.

1422
01:04:17.600 --> 01:04:21.400
These are the moments in our show where I'm like, hey, babe, how was your day?

1423
01:04:21.480 --> 01:04:22.040
What did you do today?

1424
01:04:22.740 --> 01:04:25.640
Well, I had to find three fake dead birds.

1425
01:04:26.620 --> 01:04:26.900
Yeah.

1426
01:04:27.360 --> 01:04:28.120
I scoured.

1427
01:04:28.120 --> 01:04:34.020
Or like when Greg goes home and his wife, Susanna, is like, hey, hon, how'd it go shooting today?

1428
01:04:34.020 --> 01:04:34.780
He's like, great.

1429
01:04:34.780 --> 01:04:36.000
I picked a dead bird.

1430
01:04:36.280 --> 01:04:36.620
Yeah.

1431
01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:38.360
I picked the yellow one.

1432
01:04:38.500 --> 01:04:39.900
I made my dead bird selection.

1433
01:04:40.720 --> 01:04:41.820
Yes, exactly.

1434
01:04:42.380 --> 01:04:46.200
Well, Michael then tells the staff, attention, attention.

1435
01:04:46.220 --> 01:04:49.220
We are holding a funeral for this bird at 4 p.m.

1436
01:04:49.360 --> 01:04:50.180
in the parking lot.

1437
01:04:50.400 --> 01:04:50.780
Be there.

1438
01:04:51.080 --> 01:04:51.840
It's on.

1439
01:04:52.260 --> 01:04:54.980
Jenna, now I think it's time to check back in on Jim and Karen.

1440
01:04:56.240 --> 01:04:57.140
Oh, yes.

1441
01:04:57.800 --> 01:04:59.260
Have they found the chips?

1442
01:05:00.120 --> 01:05:00.920
What's the status?

1443
01:05:01.280 --> 01:05:06.200
Where are they in their search for this very hard to find potato chip bag?

1444
01:05:06.820 --> 01:05:08.520
That's actually in the vending machine.

1445
01:05:08.560 --> 01:05:09.960
That might be in the vending machine.

1446
01:05:10.360 --> 01:05:11.980
That is in the vending machine.

1447
01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:17.140
That, according to a fan catch, is B1 in the vending machine.

1448
01:05:17.500 --> 01:05:19.160
Yeah, it's right there waiting for them.

1449
01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:26.680
Well, Karen kind of impresses Jim because she decides to call Montreal, the Montreal factory,

1450
01:05:26.680 --> 01:05:27.780
to see if they have them.

1451
01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:29.440
And she speaks French.

1452
01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:30.580
May we.

1453
01:05:30.780 --> 01:05:31.540
May we.

1454
01:05:31.660 --> 01:05:32.180
May we.

1455
01:05:32.320 --> 01:05:34.300
A lot of people wrote in about this.

1456
01:05:34.740 --> 01:05:39.940
Brianna Bullard and many, many others want to know, does Rashida Jones actually know French?

1457
01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:42.480
So I texted Rashida.

1458
01:05:42.640 --> 01:05:48.100
Since we're already texting about her breakfast order, she's probably like, Angela, what is happening?

1459
01:05:49.400 --> 01:05:50.900
This is what she said.

1460
01:05:51.080 --> 01:05:54.640
She goes, no, at this time, I didn't know French.

1461
01:05:54.640 --> 01:05:57.520
I worked really hard on that line.

1462
01:05:57.580 --> 01:06:04.640
But a few years later, I ended up going to France to do a French immersion school in the south of France.

1463
01:06:05.140 --> 01:06:09.000
Maybe this scene was the beginning and it got me inspired.

1464
01:06:10.140 --> 01:06:10.700
Wow.

1465
01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:12.220
That's crazy.

1466
01:06:12.220 --> 01:06:14.820
She did not speak French, but now she does.

1467
01:06:15.140 --> 01:06:15.500
Yes.

1468
01:06:15.600 --> 01:06:19.160
I was like, I want to go to a French immersion class in the south of France.

1469
01:06:19.160 --> 01:06:20.400
That sounds amazing.

1470
01:06:21.300 --> 01:06:21.700
Same.

1471
01:06:22.820 --> 01:06:23.220
Same.

1472
01:06:23.220 --> 01:06:24.520
I took French in high school.

1473
01:06:25.020 --> 01:06:25.560
You did?

1474
01:06:25.820 --> 01:06:28.080
Oh, I have a story about that, actually.

1475
01:06:28.360 --> 01:06:28.680
Yeah.

1476
01:06:28.700 --> 01:06:30.120
Can I tell this real quickly?

1477
01:06:30.380 --> 01:06:30.640
Yeah.

1478
01:06:30.840 --> 01:06:32.980
I took French in high school from Sister Landry.

1479
01:06:33.160 --> 01:06:34.320
I went to a Catholic school.

1480
01:06:34.480 --> 01:06:36.300
Sister Landry was my French teacher.

1481
01:06:37.100 --> 01:06:42.900
And I dreamed, I told you guys this before, I dreamed from the time I was a little girl that I would one day go to Paris.

1482
01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:45.520
And then in my 30s, it finally happened.

1483
01:06:45.660 --> 01:06:48.840
Of course, at this point, I was now very far from my French training.

1484
01:06:49.880 --> 01:06:51.480
I couldn't really speak French.

1485
01:06:51.800 --> 01:06:54.460
But Lee and I, we went to a little farmer's market.

1486
01:06:54.540 --> 01:06:58.040
And I guess in France, you don't touch the fruit.

1487
01:06:58.240 --> 01:06:58.820
Uh-oh.

1488
01:06:58.980 --> 01:06:59.200
Okay?

1489
01:06:59.500 --> 01:07:02.220
You don't select your own produce.

1490
01:07:02.720 --> 01:07:08.440
You point to it or you trust the person selling the produce to select the best pieces for you.

1491
01:07:09.480 --> 01:07:10.900
I didn't know this.

1492
01:07:11.020 --> 01:07:11.280
Okay?

1493
01:07:11.640 --> 01:07:12.640
Oh, my gosh.

1494
01:07:12.780 --> 01:07:16.480
I would have been, I pick up things, I thump them, I smell them.

1495
01:07:16.480 --> 01:07:17.620
No, lady.

1496
01:07:17.800 --> 01:07:18.080
Uh-oh.

1497
01:07:18.080 --> 01:07:18.600
Don't do it.

1498
01:07:18.640 --> 01:07:18.920
Uh-oh.

1499
01:07:18.920 --> 01:07:22.340
If you go to a farmer's market in France, here's my hot tip for you.

1500
01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:23.680
Do not touch that fruit.

1501
01:07:23.960 --> 01:07:24.900
Don't thump the melon.

1502
01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:26.320
Don't thump the melon in France.

1503
01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:26.760
Okay?

1504
01:07:26.920 --> 01:07:27.160
Okay.

1505
01:07:27.440 --> 01:07:32.920
Now, all through this trip, we'd been going to restaurants and we'd been going to tourist places.

1506
01:07:32.920 --> 01:07:35.240
And I was just fumbling with my French.

1507
01:07:35.360 --> 01:07:36.520
I couldn't get anything out.

1508
01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:39.840
I could read the menus, but I couldn't order anything.

1509
01:07:40.220 --> 01:07:43.680
I was, I thought I was going to really help us, but I couldn't help us.

1510
01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:44.640
But here we are.

1511
01:07:44.760 --> 01:07:46.000
We're at this little market.

1512
01:07:46.220 --> 01:07:47.920
I have done the faux pas.

1513
01:07:48.200 --> 01:07:49.200
I believe that's French.

1514
01:07:49.380 --> 01:07:51.520
The faux pas of touching the fruit.

1515
01:07:52.020 --> 01:07:57.980
And this woman turns to her co-worker and goes off on me.

1516
01:07:57.980 --> 01:08:04.060
She, in French, is calling me, like, kind of like a dumb American.

1517
01:08:04.520 --> 01:08:04.920
You're a piece of trash.

1518
01:08:04.920 --> 01:08:10.240
Yeah, basically, whatever it was, just these Americans come here and they touch our fruit.

1519
01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:12.340
She was, like, going on and on, okay?

1520
01:08:12.340 --> 01:08:13.300
In French.

1521
01:08:15.040 --> 01:08:21.520
I was this mixture of just, like, embarrassed, but also felt like, I'm sorry.

1522
01:08:21.700 --> 01:08:23.720
How was I supposed to know that?

1523
01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:26.020
I'm a nice person.

1524
01:08:27.080 --> 01:08:29.140
I, I'm not a bad person.

1525
01:08:29.140 --> 01:08:31.080
I'm sorry I picked up that banana.

1526
01:08:31.660 --> 01:08:38.340
And Angela, this fluent French spilled out of my mouth at this woman.

1527
01:08:38.480 --> 01:08:44.920
And in French, I suddenly had the command to say, I'm so sorry, but in the United States,

1528
01:08:44.920 --> 01:08:46.540
we pick our own fruit.

1529
01:08:46.680 --> 01:08:48.000
I didn't know about your custom.

1530
01:08:48.000 --> 01:08:51.840
I apologize, but you don't need to say nasty things about me.

1531
01:08:51.840 --> 01:08:58.360
And the look on her face was like, oh, because she certainly didn't think that the fruit-touching

1532
01:08:58.360 --> 01:09:01.180
American could speak French or understand French.

1533
01:09:01.320 --> 01:09:05.800
And by the way, I didn't know that I had such a command of the language until I was insulted

1534
01:09:05.800 --> 01:09:08.000
in a farmer's market in Paris.

1535
01:09:08.180 --> 01:09:09.700
And then your superpower came out.

1536
01:09:10.100 --> 01:09:10.600
Yes.

1537
01:09:10.740 --> 01:09:15.040
You know how, like, some, some superheroes, their power doesn't come through until they're

1538
01:09:15.040 --> 01:09:15.700
in crisis.

1539
01:09:16.340 --> 01:09:19.400
I was like a mom lifting a car off her baby.

1540
01:09:19.700 --> 01:09:19.860
Yeah, exactly.

1541
01:09:20.200 --> 01:09:20.500
All right.

1542
01:09:20.640 --> 01:09:25.940
So now we're back at Dunder Mifflin and Kelly is crying by the copier in the annex.

1543
01:09:26.380 --> 01:09:26.600
Yeah.

1544
01:09:26.820 --> 01:09:27.720
She is in tears.

1545
01:09:28.360 --> 01:09:34.020
Michael thinks he's finally made a breakthrough until she reveals that she's really crying

1546
01:09:34.020 --> 01:09:35.340
about her plans with Ryan.

1547
01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:38.740
I have a question about this scene.

1548
01:09:38.860 --> 01:09:39.000
Okay.

1549
01:09:40.060 --> 01:09:42.880
What is up with this second copier in the annex?

1550
01:09:43.220 --> 01:09:44.480
Is it always there?

1551
01:09:44.760 --> 01:09:46.540
I have never seen it before.

1552
01:09:47.140 --> 01:09:48.180
What is happening?

1553
01:09:48.360 --> 01:09:51.560
I think they just rolled it in from the bullpen for the scene.

1554
01:09:52.300 --> 01:09:53.600
I feel like they did.

1555
01:09:53.600 --> 01:09:57.700
And they did it because in the background, in the break room, Dwight is in there.

1556
01:09:57.860 --> 01:10:02.620
You can see him over Kelly's shoulder trying to shove this bird into an empty soda can.

1557
01:10:02.620 --> 01:10:06.880
I feel like that's why they had to put the copier there to combine these two moments

1558
01:10:06.880 --> 01:10:09.200
to have a reason for Michael to be back there.

1559
01:10:09.360 --> 01:10:09.500
Right.

1560
01:10:09.600 --> 01:10:12.040
And for him to see over his shoulder and all of that.

1561
01:10:12.280 --> 01:10:12.680
Exactly.

1562
01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:15.180
That copier did not live there in that hallway.

1563
01:10:15.420 --> 01:10:16.760
They just shoved it there.

1564
01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:17.100
Yeah.

1565
01:10:17.280 --> 01:10:18.480
I did not think so.

1566
01:10:18.680 --> 01:10:20.520
I feel like I will track this.

1567
01:10:20.660 --> 01:10:24.040
It's going to be a soft track, but I am going to look for this copier more.

1568
01:10:24.180 --> 01:10:29.220
I think it should be a very soft track because I would be bold enough to say I think it

1569
01:10:29.220 --> 01:10:31.100
might be the only time it's in the annex.

1570
01:10:31.100 --> 01:10:37.860
I know that's setting myself up for some fan mail of Angela actually in season eight, but

1571
01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:42.100
I'm just saying I don't think that copier visits the annex very often.

1572
01:10:42.420 --> 01:10:42.900
All right.

1573
01:10:42.980 --> 01:10:47.780
But this leads us into one of my favorite Dwight talking heads where he explains he's

1574
01:10:47.780 --> 01:10:50.640
justifying why is he shoving this bird into a can.

1575
01:10:50.820 --> 01:10:53.740
And it's because he grew up on a farm.

1576
01:10:53.960 --> 01:10:54.100
Yeah.

1577
01:10:54.100 --> 01:10:58.480
And guys, they reburied his grandfather in an old oil drum.

1578
01:11:00.460 --> 01:11:01.540
OK, hold on.

1579
01:11:01.660 --> 01:11:06.120
I mean, the same the same grandfather that was buried in the suit that he wore.

1580
01:11:06.320 --> 01:11:07.020
Like, what's happening?

1581
01:11:07.140 --> 01:11:09.340
How many times has this guy been brought up out of the ground?

1582
01:11:09.580 --> 01:11:11.600
This is one of my favorite things about our show.

1583
01:11:11.960 --> 01:11:12.640
Teresa S.

1584
01:11:12.940 --> 01:11:13.440
also loves it.

1585
01:11:13.440 --> 01:11:18.620
She wrote in to say, in Casino Night, Dwight says that his grandfather was buried in the

1586
01:11:18.620 --> 01:11:19.720
tuxedo he's wearing.

1587
01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:20.100
Yeah.

1588
01:11:20.820 --> 01:11:24.860
And in grief counseling, he said his grandfather was reburied in an oil drum.

1589
01:11:25.020 --> 01:11:25.160
Yeah.

1590
01:11:25.780 --> 01:11:26.300
Reburied?

1591
01:11:26.460 --> 01:11:26.600
What?

1592
01:11:26.660 --> 01:11:28.160
She wrote, reburied.

1593
01:11:28.160 --> 01:11:28.660
What happened?

1594
01:11:29.140 --> 01:11:31.680
What is the story behind Dwight's grandfather?

1595
01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:33.460
Was he exhumed for some reason?

1596
01:11:33.460 --> 01:11:38.800
And did Dwight take the tuxedo off his dead grandfather before reburying him?

1597
01:11:38.880 --> 01:11:39.820
And then fold him up.

1598
01:11:39.860 --> 01:11:40.240
It sounds like it.

1599
01:11:40.380 --> 01:11:42.680
Then he folded him up and tried to shove him in a drum.

1600
01:11:43.060 --> 01:11:45.040
Teresa, that's exactly what it sounds like.

1601
01:11:45.380 --> 01:11:47.840
This is some of my favorite writing on our show.

1602
01:11:47.840 --> 01:11:52.260
In that moment in Casino Night, when Dwight just says he's wearing a tuxedo, my grandfather

1603
01:11:52.260 --> 01:11:53.060
was buried in it.

1604
01:11:53.200 --> 01:11:53.780
That's it.

1605
01:11:53.880 --> 01:11:54.980
We say no more about it.

1606
01:11:55.120 --> 01:11:59.400
And then three episodes later, he's like, when my grandfather was reburied.

1607
01:12:00.220 --> 01:12:02.920
And it's like, slowly, it's all coming together.

1608
01:12:03.780 --> 01:12:04.460
Sort of.

1609
01:12:04.620 --> 01:12:05.060
Sort of.

1610
01:12:05.120 --> 01:12:05.740
Not completely.

1611
01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:08.940
It's one of my favorite bits of how our show is written.

1612
01:12:09.300 --> 01:12:11.160
So now we're at reception, right?

1613
01:12:11.320 --> 01:12:14.740
And Pam has prepared this really beautiful box for the bird funeral.

1614
01:12:14.920 --> 01:12:16.440
I mean, it is amazing.

1615
01:12:16.440 --> 01:12:17.900
The attention to detail.

1616
01:12:18.340 --> 01:12:21.620
She asked Dwight if he would perhaps want to play a song.

1617
01:12:21.740 --> 01:12:22.900
She's like, do you have your recorder?

1618
01:12:23.040 --> 01:12:25.380
He's like, always, always has.

1619
01:12:25.640 --> 01:12:25.720
Always.

1620
01:12:27.540 --> 01:12:31.200
And, you know, Pam has this really sweet talking head where she's like, well, you know

1621
01:12:31.200 --> 01:12:31.440
what?

1622
01:12:31.440 --> 01:12:35.460
I didn't anticipate I'd be throwing a bird funeral today, but you never know, working

1623
01:12:35.460 --> 01:12:35.820
here.

1624
01:12:36.020 --> 01:12:36.640
That's right.

1625
01:12:36.760 --> 01:12:38.560
We had a fan question from Andrew S.

1626
01:12:38.680 --> 01:12:43.260
Did Jenna make the box for the bird funeral herself, or was that a Phil Shea creation?

1627
01:12:43.640 --> 01:12:46.160
Either way, how was it constructed?

1628
01:12:46.480 --> 01:12:48.160
Well, guys, I did not make it.

1629
01:12:48.500 --> 01:12:49.720
Phil Shea made it.

1630
01:12:49.720 --> 01:12:55.480
He said he actually made a few options and presented them again to Jen and Roger and Greg.

1631
01:12:55.680 --> 01:13:00.340
They picked the one that they liked, and then Phil had to make multiples of it because, you

1632
01:13:00.340 --> 01:13:02.260
know, it gets set on fire.

1633
01:13:02.440 --> 01:13:02.660
Yeah.

1634
01:13:02.660 --> 01:13:04.480
So he had to make a whole bunch of those.

1635
01:13:04.800 --> 01:13:08.060
I looked it up in the script, and it was described in the script.

1636
01:13:08.060 --> 01:13:10.240
So here's what Phil had to go off of.

1637
01:13:10.440 --> 01:13:15.820
It said, it is a hollowed out tissue box with tissue padding in the center for the bird

1638
01:13:15.820 --> 01:13:16.520
to lie on.

1639
01:13:16.620 --> 01:13:21.600
And the outside is decorated in tissue paper and has a tiny little toothpick cross.

1640
01:13:22.080 --> 01:13:24.060
My favorite part are the...

1641
01:13:24.060 --> 01:13:24.940
The handles.

1642
01:13:24.940 --> 01:13:28.240
The handles made out of the pencils.

1643
01:13:28.640 --> 01:13:30.180
So that was a Phil addition.

1644
01:13:30.580 --> 01:13:32.080
That was just Phil.

1645
01:13:32.440 --> 01:13:32.720
Yeah.

1646
01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:33.300
Yeah.

1647
01:13:33.520 --> 01:13:39.960
He needed to make sure that tiny bird casket had little bars for the pallbearers.

1648
01:13:40.420 --> 01:13:41.220
That's right.

1649
01:13:41.480 --> 01:13:43.160
The tiny pallbearers.

1650
01:13:43.280 --> 01:13:43.680
Yes.

1651
01:13:43.900 --> 01:13:47.080
We are going to wrap things up in Stanford now, guys.

1652
01:13:47.360 --> 01:13:48.420
Jim found the chips.

1653
01:13:49.060 --> 01:13:50.440
He left them on her desk.

1654
01:13:50.560 --> 01:13:52.100
He's quite pleased with himself.

1655
01:13:52.440 --> 01:13:52.720
Yeah.

1656
01:13:52.720 --> 01:13:56.380
I really wanted my mom to record a sting that said, flirty Jim.

1657
01:13:56.620 --> 01:13:59.700
But we couldn't get it together.

1658
01:14:00.340 --> 01:14:02.560
Well, these scenes drove people crazy.

1659
01:14:02.980 --> 01:14:07.060
I went back and looked at some of the comments from when this aired.

1660
01:14:08.600 --> 01:14:10.160
Here is what they said.

1661
01:14:11.060 --> 01:14:14.140
D-Temp said, I feel kind of weird about Jim and Karen.

1662
01:14:14.980 --> 01:14:17.740
But Hartman said, the Karen thing?

1663
01:14:17.980 --> 01:14:19.860
Why am I liking it?

1664
01:14:19.940 --> 01:14:20.680
But I am.

1665
01:14:21.080 --> 01:14:21.340
Whoa.

1666
01:14:22.040 --> 01:14:24.520
I remember people freaking out about it.

1667
01:14:25.440 --> 01:14:28.740
Well, Keeley said, Jim and Karen have great chemistry.

1668
01:14:29.500 --> 01:14:31.800
And Mandy said, okay, I'm sorry.

1669
01:14:31.940 --> 01:14:35.940
I can't believe there are actually people out there who are okay with Jim hooking up with Karen.

1670
01:14:36.140 --> 01:14:37.020
That's what I remember.

1671
01:14:38.080 --> 01:14:39.280
But Kim E.

1672
01:14:39.520 --> 01:14:40.300
said this.

1673
01:14:41.220 --> 01:14:41.540
Hmm.

1674
01:14:41.940 --> 01:14:44.240
Jim likes to buy chips for all his ladies.

1675
01:14:45.700 --> 01:14:47.320
And I'm with you, Kim.

1676
01:14:48.400 --> 01:14:51.340
As Pam, I'm like, oh, is this what you do?

1677
01:14:51.720 --> 01:14:54.700
You just find all the girls their favorite chips?

1678
01:14:55.080 --> 01:14:55.220
Yeah.

1679
01:14:55.480 --> 01:14:56.280
That's his game.

1680
01:14:57.160 --> 01:14:58.400
Are we saying that right?

1681
01:14:58.660 --> 01:14:59.020
Oh.

1682
01:14:59.340 --> 01:15:00.000
Is that your game?

1683
01:15:00.160 --> 01:15:00.420
You and I?

1684
01:15:00.680 --> 01:15:02.100
I 100% doubt we are.

1685
01:15:02.100 --> 01:15:02.480
I'm sure we are.

1686
01:15:02.520 --> 01:15:03.980
Oh, Sam's giving us an okay.

1687
01:15:03.980 --> 01:15:04.920
Sam gave us an okay.

1688
01:15:05.120 --> 01:15:06.020
That's his game.

1689
01:15:06.780 --> 01:15:07.460
All right.

1690
01:15:07.600 --> 01:15:09.280
Should we go to this bird funeral, Angela?

1691
01:15:09.560 --> 01:15:10.440
I can't wait.

1692
01:15:11.000 --> 01:15:12.340
Please take me there.

1693
01:15:12.340 --> 01:15:17.280
Well, we're out in the driveway, really, next to the warehouse.

1694
01:15:17.660 --> 01:15:23.100
This was an actual parking lot that we used, and it was right next to the warehouse, and

1695
01:15:23.100 --> 01:15:24.240
that's where we filmed this.

1696
01:15:25.000 --> 01:15:28.220
Michael is very moved by Pam's beautiful coffin.

1697
01:15:28.760 --> 01:15:34.940
It starts with Pam saying a few words about the bird, but we slowly realize that Pam is speaking

1698
01:15:34.940 --> 01:15:37.560
about the bird, but she's speaking to Michael.

1699
01:15:37.880 --> 01:15:40.660
About his feelings about death and loss.

1700
01:15:40.660 --> 01:15:43.020
And about his aloneness.

1701
01:15:43.720 --> 01:15:50.480
She talks a lot about how just because the bird was alone doesn't mean he was unloved,

1702
01:15:50.680 --> 01:15:54.700
that he was very loved and respected by the other birds.

1703
01:15:54.960 --> 01:15:56.420
And then she sings a song.

1704
01:15:56.640 --> 01:15:58.880
I was very surprised by this for Pam.

1705
01:15:59.140 --> 01:16:00.660
She really put herself out there.

1706
01:16:00.780 --> 01:16:02.780
She sings On the Wings of Love.

1707
01:16:03.060 --> 01:16:05.340
She's accompanied by Dwight on the recorder.

1708
01:16:05.960 --> 01:16:07.440
Michael is very moved.

1709
01:16:07.560 --> 01:16:08.440
He's holding hands.

1710
01:16:08.440 --> 01:16:09.300
He's swaying.

1711
01:16:09.420 --> 01:16:10.340
He's tearing up.

1712
01:16:10.660 --> 01:16:12.920
We got a lot of questions about this scene.

1713
01:16:13.800 --> 01:16:14.640
Richard H.

1714
01:16:14.900 --> 01:16:15.960
really covers them all.

1715
01:16:16.240 --> 01:16:19.280
He says, did Rain already know how to play the recorder?

1716
01:16:19.680 --> 01:16:21.860
Did Jenna sing the entire song?

1717
01:16:22.020 --> 01:16:24.520
Or did the singing end where the scene was cut?

1718
01:16:25.160 --> 01:16:28.140
He also wanted to know, did we rehearse our duet?

1719
01:16:28.920 --> 01:16:30.280
Here's what I can tell you.

1720
01:16:31.360 --> 01:16:33.460
Yes, Rain knew how to play the recorder.

1721
01:16:34.200 --> 01:16:36.400
Remember, he played it in Take Your Daughter to Work Day.

1722
01:16:36.400 --> 01:16:37.940
He was actually very good.

1723
01:16:38.220 --> 01:16:41.980
But listen to all of the instruments that Rain can play in real life, Angela.

1724
01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:54.100
He can play the recorder, piano, clarinet, saxophone, bassoon, guitar, drums, xylophone, bells, and a little tuba and baritone.

1725
01:16:54.100 --> 01:16:56.180
What are you, a one-man band, Rain?

1726
01:16:56.620 --> 01:16:57.760
He kind of is.

1727
01:16:57.920 --> 01:17:01.660
I knew you could play the bassoon because your book, The Bassoon King, is great.

1728
01:17:01.660 --> 01:17:02.820
So I knew that.

1729
01:17:03.240 --> 01:17:06.000
But I didn't know you were a walking one-man show.

1730
01:17:06.320 --> 01:17:08.760
Suffice it to say, Rain knew his stuff.

1731
01:17:09.460 --> 01:17:13.860
And we did rehearse, but just a little bit right before the scene.

1732
01:17:14.300 --> 01:17:15.660
Rain and I practiced separately.

1733
01:17:15.960 --> 01:17:19.000
I had listened to the song over and over again to memorize the words.

1734
01:17:19.160 --> 01:17:22.640
I had to sing the first verse through the chorus.

1735
01:17:23.400 --> 01:17:25.320
That's what I had to do over and over again.

1736
01:17:25.940 --> 01:17:27.380
I was so nervous.

1737
01:17:28.200 --> 01:17:29.560
Well, I thought you did fantastic.

1738
01:17:30.640 --> 01:17:31.440
Thank you.

1739
01:17:31.680 --> 01:17:33.880
I have a fan question from the scene, Jenna.

1740
01:17:34.480 --> 01:17:34.940
Okay.

1741
01:17:35.500 --> 01:17:40.680
So Chelsea wrote in and said, was Angela's A-man improvised?

1742
01:17:40.900 --> 01:17:45.280
A lot of people wrote in about this because it was so quick after Pam is done talking.

